    <Axiom id="AX_SPEC_MANDATORY_DIAGRAM">
      The diagram floor is a fixed ladder by decomposition stage, not one "when useful" Overview
      picture — the same discipline agent-inbox's `AX_COMPREHENSION_LADDER` applies to a review
      applies here to a design, at authoring time instead of review time. Six rungs plus a
      refine/pivot-only Delta rung, each answering ONE distinct question — no two rungs answer the
      same thing; the worked example lives in
      `ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/module-diagram-ladder.example.md`:
      1. **Overview** (module spec) — WHAT this module does, main happy-path flow. Unconditional.
         Held by tooling: `sdd-check` fails a missing section (`SDD_NO_DIAGRAM_BLOCK`) or an empty
         one (`SDD_DIAGRAM_BLOCK_EMPTY`). The `Inter-Module Dependencies` graph is machine-parsed
         and does NOT satisfy this rung.
      2. **Module Map** (scope spec, `STEP_1_MODULE_MAP`) — WHERE each module sits along the
         decomposition axes (data/domain ↔ UI ↔ infrastructure, `AX_SEPARATION_OF_CONCERNS`).
         Unconditional whenever ≥2 modules exist.
      3. **Entity Inventory** (`STEP_2_ENTITY_INVENTORY`) — HOW entities relate (ER sketch).
         Conditional: ≥2 entities in one module connected by reference/ownership/composition. A
         single entity, or a set with no real relation, states that explicitly and skips it.
      4. **Entity Surface** (`STEP_3_ENTITY_SURFACE`) — the entity's LIFECYCLE, when it is a real
         state machine (≥3 distinct states/transitions, not just create→terminate). Conditional,
         per entity.
      5. **Module Contracts** (`STEP_4_CONTRACTS_DBC`) — the dependency GRAPH among Ports /
         Adapters / Services. Mandatory whenever the module has ≥1 Port or Adapter; a Service-only
         module states so explicitly instead of a silently missing graph — floor, never nothing.
      6. **Call Chain** (`STEP_4_CONTRACTS_DBC`, right after rung 5 — new per the 2026-08-20
         visualization-chain decision) — WHO calls WHOM, in what ORDER, for the module's one main
         scenario: a `sequenceDiagram`, or its table-of-steps equivalent (step / participant /
         action / data, `CALL_CHAIN_FORMAT` in `formats/dbc-contracts.xml`) — both forms count
         equally, neither is a fallback for the other. Mandatory whenever the module's inventory
         lists ≥2 abstractions (entities and/or Ports/Adapters/Services combined); a module below
         that threshold states so explicitly.
      Each rung is drawn as ASCII in chat at the step that owns it, BEFORE that step's Approval
      Check — a diagram appearing for the first time in the written spec means the operator never
      saw the shape they are approving (`AX_CHAT_BEFORE_SPEC`); an Approval Check without it is the
      same class of violation as `H_ASK_BEFORE_FORMAT` (asked before shown), and the operator may
      reject and demand it. Every diagram carries a caption naming the requirement IDs it shows,
      per `DIAGRAM_CAPTION_FORMAT` (`formats/diagram-vocabulary.xml`).

      **Delta rung** (`STEP_6_FINAL_HIERARCHY`, `refine-module` / `pivot` only) — the diagram twin
      of the `CHANGE_MANIFEST` text delta: a composition graph carrying ONLY the changed corner
      with the new node marked (`:::new` or a `(добавлено)` suffix) plus the ADDED Call Chain
      steps, per `DELTA_DIAGRAM_FORMAT` (`formats/pivot-formats.xml`) — the unchanged part of the
      module is NOT redrawn. Mandatory on every `refine-module` / `pivot` round.

      This ladder (rungs 1–6 plus Delta) is the mandatory floor for NEW module specs only — a
      module spec written before rungs 6 or Delta existed is not retroactively broken by their
      absence. Beyond this floor, the author adds more views (data-flow, before/after) wherever a
      further diagram genuinely helps a reader — that part stays judgment, never a gate.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_SPEC_PROGRESSIVE_DISCLOSURE">
      A module spec reads top-down: a human-readable summary on top, heavy contract detail folded beneath. Entity Surfaces and Module Contracts wrap their body in `<details><summary>…</summary>` so a reader sees the shape without wading through every precondition. Held by tooling: `sdd-check` fails an unfolded heavy section (`SDD_SECTION_NOT_FOLDED`). This is the spec-artifact analogue of the chat-output `AX_PROGRESSIVE_DISCLOSURE` — summary first, detail on demand.
    </Axiom>

